  ],
  "knowledgePattern": "Solve a variable-limit modulus integral on both sides of its corner",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Sketch the integrand",
      "body": "<p>Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Mark its vertex, both intercepts and its height at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The absolute value reflects negative outputs before the vertical shift.",
      "pitfall": "Subtract 2 after applying the absolute value.",
      "pause": "Draw the graph accurately enough to calculate its small areas.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "An absolute value keeps a nonnegative value and reverses the sign of a negative value."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep a running area",
      "body": "<p>Move from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> along the graph. Add area above the axis and subtract area below it. Record the accumulated area at each intercept and at the vertex.</p>",
      "recap": "A definite integral is signed area.",
      "pitfall": "The integral can decrease while the graph is below the axis.",
      "pause": "Locate the sections where the running total could equal the given value.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l21",
          "reminder": "A definite integral is signed area."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the straight edges",
      "body": "<p>On the first section, express the area up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> as a trapezium. On later sections, add or subtract the relevant triangle areas.</p>",
      "recap": "Straight-line graphs give triangle and trapezium areas.",
      "pitfall": "Keep each value of k inside the section used to find it.",
      "pause": "Find all positive k and add them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l11",
          "reminder": "Calculate each trapezium from its width and its two end heights."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> has intercepts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Its signed integral from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, falls to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and falls to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the area is a trapezium: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Setting this equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> belongs to this section.</p><p>Between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> the graph is negative, so the accumulated integral decreases strictly. It equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> exactly at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, add the positive triangle to the existing total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> in this section.</p><p>The positive values are therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, whose sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>. The answer is D.</p>"
}
